# locale/en/icons -- the NAME of every icon, in the source language.
#
# A CATALOGUE FRAGMENT, not a catalogue.  Round I18N owns
# `locale/<lang>/messages`; this file is meant to be laid into it, and
# it is kept apart so that two rounds working at the same time do not
# edit the same file.  The keys are already in that round's shape:
# English, never translated, dots for structure.
#
# WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST AT ALL.  An icon is a picture, and a
# picture cannot be read out loud, cannot be searched for, and cannot be
# guessed at by somebody who has not seen it before.  A crossed circle
# means "blocked" only to a person who has already been told.  So every
# icon that appears in the interface carries a name, the name appears
# under the pointer, and the name is TEXT -- which means it has to be
# translatable, which means it belongs here and not in the drawing code.
# The skill file this round worked from says the same thing twice, under
# `aria-labels` ("aria-label for icon-only buttons") and under
# `icon-only buttons without labels` in the accessibility row.
#
# AND THE SECOND REASON, which is the harder one: FOUR OF THESE ICONS
# DIFFER BY COLOUR IN EVERY OTHER SYSTEM -- warning, error, success,
# info.  Round ICONS gave them four different OUTLINES (a triangle, a
# crossed circle, a ticked circle, a circled i) so that colour is not
# the only carrier.  The name is the third carrier, and the one that
# works when the icon is too small to read.
#
# THE KEY IS THE ICON NAME PLUS `.tip`.  That is not decoration: it
# means `assets/icons/icons.map` and this file can be checked against
# each other, and `tools/icons/run.sh` does exactly that -- an icon
# without a name and a name without an icon are both errors.

